From a86e852f4872694473a30b78ace5ec4693384d9c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 19:17:23 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 100/206] fix(analyze_snapshot): do not abort on Code objects
 with no known stub name
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Our analyze_snapshot aborted on every snapshot we produce. The default,
upstream dump mode -- no Shorebird flag involved -- hit UNREACHABLE at
analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc:355 and died with SIGABRT.

Instrumented rather than guessed: four Code objects, eight bytes each, null
owner, that are neither in VM_STUB_CODE_LIST nor isolate-specific stub copies.
Upstream's comment there says "Expected to handle all possibilities"; our
snapshots contain one it did not expect.

They get the name Dart itself gives them, code.Name(), which is
"[unknown stub]". The tool now dumps 1776 Code objects and exits 0 where it
used to abort.

⚠️ Marked in the code as a fallback and not a diagnosis. Why our snapshots
contain these and Shorebird's visibly do not is still open: every 8-byte Code
in their dump has an owner, so their binary never reaches this branch, which
also means what they would print is not observable and this name is ours.

Our own Shorebird stubs are NOT the explanation, and that was checked because
they were the obvious suspects: ResumeOnCPU, RunDartOnCPU,
CallSimulatorFromFfiTrampoline and the rest are all in VM_STUB_CODE_LIST and
get named through StubCode::ForEachStub.

--shorebird still reports zero functions; that is the separate base-address bug
from task 33 and is untouched here.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc b/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc
index 9d3358cf073..c5b2ed81c10 100644
--- a/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc
+++ b/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc
@@ -352,7 +352,19 @@ void SnapshotAnalyzer::DumpCode(const Code& code) {
                    thread_->isolate_group()->object_store(), code)) {
       js_.PrintProperty("name", stub_name);
     } else {
-      UNREACHABLE();
+      // Upstream asserts this is unreachable. It is reachable on our own AOT
+      // snapshots: four Code objects, eight bytes each, with a null owner, that
+      // are neither in VM_STUB_CODE_LIST nor isolate-specific copies. Aborting
+      // the whole dump over them makes analyze_snapshot useless against every
+      // snapshot we produce, so they get the name Dart itself gives them.
+      //
+      // ⚠️ This is a fallback, not a diagnosis. Why our snapshots contain them
+      // and Shorebird's visibly do not is still open -- their dump never
+      // reaches this branch, so what they would print here is not observable,
+      // and this name is ours. Our own Shorebird stubs are NOT the explanation:
+      // ResumeOnCPU, RunDartOnCPU and the rest are all in VM_STUB_CODE_LIST and
+      // are named through it. Checked, because they were the obvious suspects.
+      js_.PrintProperty("name", code.Name());
     }
   }
 }
